    "q-334630": {
      "dom_id": "step-q334630",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\">${e}^{At}={e}^{-t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"140px\" align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td width=\"140px\" align=\"center\">$2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$</td>\n<td width=\"30px\" align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=(-1+2\\text{i})t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=(-1-2\\text{i})t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((-1+2\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(-1+2\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(-1+2\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ (-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+2{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
