    "q-334730": {
      "dom_id": "step-q334730",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $2,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\">${e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"80px\" align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td width=\"80px\" align=\"center\">$4t$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-4t$</td>\n<td width=\"30px\" align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -4{\\alpha }_{1}t & 6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=2t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(2t)={e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(2t)={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n2t{e}^{2t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}(1-2t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-2{e}^{2t}t+{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(1-4t), \\\\ 6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =6{e}^{2t}t+{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(1+4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1-4t & 4t \\\\ -4t & 1+4t\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
